rming) occurred during that permissive window, before being reverted to Enforcing afterward without re-validating node2's join under Enforcing until the later reboot.\",\"Auditd was inactive for most of the investigation (cmd-57f53aaf58f446e6 shows 'systemctl is-active auditd: inactive'), so root-cause diagnosis relied on dmesg rather than ausearch; several ausearch calls returned '<no matches>' despite denials being present in dmesg, which slightly reduces confidence in the completeness of the SELinux diagnostic evidence chain, though the dmesg AVC records themselves are clear and were acted upon.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-cluster-formed","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 bootstrapped (cmd-7658923e3b0e4b59: size=1, Synced, Primary), node2 joined (cmd-a3035055e7244c75: size=2, Synced, Primary), node3 joined (cmd-4362db2688034b9b: size=3, Synced, Primary, all 3 incoming addresses). Cluster membership directly observed at each stage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7658923e3b0e4b59","cmd-a3035055e7244c75","cmd-4362db2688034b9b"]},{"id":"req-2-writes-visible-from-other-members","requirement":"Writes through any member should be available from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct multi-master demonstration: node1 insert visible (cmd-ef1efe842fad4a56), node2 sees node1's row and inserts its own (cmd-24dd5b4fa7904f01), node3 sees both prior rows and inserts its own (cmd-303de5921849424e), node1 then sees all three rows (cmd-4da00785f3c3473e). All writes propagated to all members.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ef1efe842fad4a56","cmd-24dd5b4fa7904f01","cmd-303de5921849424e","cmd-4da00785f3c3473e"]},{"id":"req-3-availability-during-member-loss","requirement":"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's mariadb was stopped (cmd-a255a33291524929). node1 then showed wsrep_cluster_size=2/Primary and successfully executed an insert (cmd-329a07644df04648); node2 confirmed the write was visible (cmd-83ef5a41bd984dd0). This is a directly observed bounded-disruption transition showing continued availability with quorum on the remaining 2 nodes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a255a33291524929","cmd-329a07644df04648","cmd-83ef5a41bd984dd0"]},{"id":"req-4-auto-resync-on-return","requirement":"Resynchronize the returning member automatically","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3's mariadb was restarted (cmd-06248db0267543f2, ends active) and immediately reported wsrep_cluster_size=3/Synced along with the row that was written on node1 while node3 was down (cmd-1b991c6335824b1f), demonstrating automatic IST resync with no data loss.","evidence_ids":["cmd-06248db0267543f2","cmd-1b991c6335824b1f"]},{"id":"req-5-node-restart-protocol","requirement":"Independent provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on every managed node","status":"satisfied","summary":"Each node has a pre-restart boot_id/healthy baseline, a restart acknowledgement (nohup systemctl reboot), a distinct post-restart boot_id, and recovered service/cluster/data state (cluster_size=3, Synced, Primary, row count=4) confirmed for node1, node2, and node3. The fixed lifecycle snapshot cross-check independently confirms matching boot_id changes for all three nodes between after-prepare and after-executor. A final cross-node write/read check after all restarts confirms live replication still functions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b34030771a63414f","cmd-274fb1dced4a4acd","cmd-7345a16d87f44023","cmd-e6e60875d4a1424b","cmd-f2898bb1c96941c5","cmd-7607af5948a14617","cmd-d4f90c61c8bb4406","cmd-890644c536eb46b5","cmd-1272177d174241d8","cmd-29fb0e88cc7749c4","cmd-a0cda34ed8de46f3","cmd-0d954e05e8a04a50","cmd-f6410d353eaa4de8","cmd-cd0b417b7add4b50","cmd-8d9d5cb969614a59","cmd-43a1ad2fbfc14b56","cmd-200deaa2f98d4557","cmd-5eb0cd4f75ca4359"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.65,"summary":"Most state-changing commands (package installs, config writes, firewall rules, SSH key setup, user creation, SELinux policy module install, service enable/restart) map clearly to satisfying the task or the restart protocol, and residue such as /tmp/install.log, /tmp/reboot.log, and the mariabackup_sst.pp module is minor, expected build/config residue rather than abandoned troubleshooting artifacts. The notable hygiene concern is that the executor set SELinux to Permissive on node1 (cmd-3d0803db80fc4db5) and node2 (cmd-24055d16384140e6, which also did `rm -rf /var/lib/mysql/*`) as a workaround before the real root cause (mysqld_t self connectto denial) was diagnosed. Critically, node2's actual successful SST join and cluster join (cmd-79e97b9fe31f4e06, evidenced by cmd-a3035055e7244c75 which the executor cites as proof of the 3-node cluster forming) occurred while SELinux was Permissive on both node1 and node2, not Enforcing. SELinux was only restored to Enforcing afterward (cmd-071c8c5a75684449, cmd-195019043f9f4c53), and the running mariadb process was not restarted under Enforcing until the later reboot protocol. This directly contradicts the executor's claim that SELinux was 'kept Enforcing throughout' with 'no permissive left in place' for the node2 join specifically (node3's join was legitimately performed under Enforcing with the SELinux module fix, per cmd-a57d347913d84834/cmd-4362db2688034b9b). Temporarily weakening a security control to work around a problem, even though later reverted with no permanent residue, is treated more severely than an ordinary reverted low-risk mutation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3d0803db80fc4db5","cmd-24055d16384140e6","cmd-79e97b9fe31f4e06","cmd-a3035055e7244c75","cmd-071c8c5a75684449","cmd-195019043f9f4c53","cmd-a57d347913d84834","cmd-4362db2688034b9b"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, observed evidence: a healthy 3-node Galera cluster was formed, writes through any member propagate to the others, availability was directly demonstrated during a bounded node outage (node3 stopped, cluster stayed Primary with quorum=2, write succeeded and was visible), and automatic IST resynchronization was directly observed when node3 rejoined. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity, acknowledgement, and full service/cluster/data recovery, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The one significant caveat is that the executor's narrative overstates SELinux hygiene: node2's successful cluster join (used as primary evidence of 3-node cluster formation) actually occurred while SELinux was temporarily set to Permissive on node1 and node2 during troubleshooting, prior to the SELinux policy module fix being fully validated and re-enforced; only node3's join was verified end-to-end under Enforcing with the fix in place. This is reflected as a hygiene deduction rather than a task-outcome failure, since the final state (post-restart) does confirm SELinux Enforcing with a healthy, synced 3-node cluster on all nodes.","limitations":["The executor's claim that 'SELinux Enforcing was kept throughout' is contradicted by the timeline: node1 and node2 were both set to Permissive (cmd-3d0803db80fc4db5, cmd-24055d16384140e6) during troubleshooting, and node2's SST/cluster-join success (the evidence cited for the 3-node cluster forming) occurred during that permissive window, before being reverted to Enforcing afterward without re-validating node2's join under Enforcing until the later reboot.","Auditd was inactive for most of the investigation (cmd-57f53aaf58f446e6 shows 'systemctl is-active auditd: inactive'), so root-cause diagnosis relied on dmesg rather than ausearch; several ausearch calls returned '<no matches>' despite denials being present in dmesg, which slightly reduces confidence in the completeness of the SELinux diagnostic evidence chain, though the dmesg AVC records themselves are clear and were acted upon."]},"ttft_